# =============================================================================
# DEEPER — the demo
# =============================================================================
#
#     loom run assets/games/deeper_demo.loom --play
#
# That is the whole thing. `--play` is not optional: without it `run` opens the
# read-only viewer, paused, and every `Hud` here is `only_in_play`, so you get a
# frozen diorama with no instructions on it. `--play` alone builds no editor
# panels; add `--edit` if you want them.
#
# **You do not start on the deck.** `--play` opens on the title screen: the rig
# seen from 50 m off with the sun behind it, the word DEEPER over the water,
# Start and Quit, and **the controls along the bottom** — which is where they
# live, because there is no Options item and deliberately so. There is no
# settings system in this tree, and an Options that opens nothing is a promise
# broken on the second screen a player ever sees. So the one thing a player
# would have opened it for is on the first screen instead.
#
# Escape there closes the window, which the screen says out loud. Start runs a
# 1.55-second curtain — half a second to black, a quarter of black while the
# world is built, then eight tenths back up on your own eyes — and the window
# opens *black* and lifts into the shot, so the half second this scene takes to
# load happens behind a curtain rather than as a stall. The buttons are drawn
# through that opening lift but do not answer until it finishes: they are
# invisible under it, and a click nobody could see was one had no business
# starting the game. See THE FRONT END at the foot of this file for the camera
# and the three lines of text, and `loom_cli::hud` for the curtain.
#
# **Nothing in it is simulated.** The front end is a camera, a rectangle and two
# buttons; the sea moves behind it because the water's clock is the wall clock
# and always has been. Two consequences worth writing down: the shot is *not*
# `--play`'s only new state — the viewer now sits with no game running for as
# long as the player leaves it there, with the file watcher live — and adding
# the front end's four nodes **does** move `World::state_hash`, which eats every
# node's name and transform. It moves no pixel and no gameplay assertion, both
# checked, and no gate pins this scene's hash. "A camera cannot affect
# determinism" was said once here and was false.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOU HAVE A BODY, AND THERE IS A GLASS TO SEE IT IN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `Rig/Player/Body` is `assets/prefabs/deckhand.loom` — 44 nodes, 28 rigid mesh
# leaves, 4,396 triangles, and thirteen `rhai` scripts that bend it. Loom has no
# skeletal animation, so he is an articulated puppet, and **that is not a
# compromise: ADR 0072.** Every ray in this engine sees a `Deform`ed or skinned
# surface at its REST pose (ADR 0062:85), so rigid parts on a node hierarchy are
# the only animation method here that a reflection can show moving.
#
# **The mirror is on the shed's north face, 1.7 m due south of where you spawn,
# and you spawn facing away from it.** Turn round. The HUD says so for the first
# 3.5 m, because a mirror nobody walks past is not a feature and this scene
# cannot be authored to open facing it — the berth is on -Z precisely because
# neither an authored eye yaw nor an authored character yaw expresses "start
# facing this way" (see the block below on `Play::start`).
#
# It is two nodes. `metallic = 1.0, roughness = 0.0` on a `box`, reflected by
# the one traced ray ADR 0019 already spends on every opaque fragment. No planar
# pass, no second camera, no mirrored projection. The glass, the frame ordering
# and the four rules that go with them are documented at `Rig/Mirror` below.
#
# **To change the way he walks, edit ONE block**: `>>> DECKHAND KNOBS` at the
# top of `assets/scripts/deckhand_gait.rhai`. Seventeen named numbers —
# `walk_leg`, `walk_knee`, `walk_bob`, `idle_breath` — published into `state`
# and read by every joint. The same block is duplicated verbatim in
# `deeper_rules.rhai` (a scene gets one `GameRules` and rhai has no import here)
# and `the_two_copies_of_the_deckhand_knobs_agree` fails when they drift.
#
# `assets/test/deckhand_walk.loom` is the walk on its own and
# `assets/test/deckhand_mirror.loom` is him and his reflection in one frame.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WEATHER — five rungs, and every number is in the ladder below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **It is a normal morning at the rig, and it gets worse the further out you
# go.** There is no clock and no schedule: the weather is a function of where
# the boat is. `deeper_rules.rhai` eases `state.dread` from the distance off
# the rig, and both the wind and the rain are rungs on that one scalar, blended
# on the fixed tick. Turn for home and it eases back down.
#
# **To change the weather, edit the five stages below. There is nothing else.**
# Each names a `wind_speed` in m/s and a `rain_intensity` in mm/h beside its
# look, so a rung is one weather and not two. Measured on the shipped scene,
# holding W from the berth:
#
#     stage        dread   wind   rain   Hs      at tick    what it is
#     inshore       0.00    3.0    0.0   0.17 m        1    a flat calm morning
#     underway      0.25    4.5    1.5   0.26 m     ~1660    a breeze, first spits
#     outofsight    0.50    7.0    6.0   0.40 m     ~2100    steady rain, chop
#     grounds       0.80   11.5   16.0   0.66 m     ~2650    heavy rain, a swell
#     theedge       1.00   16.0   30.0   0.91 m     ~3400    a gale and a downpour
#
# `Hs` is significant wave height, read off the scene itself with
# `loom water --at 40,-10 --sim <tick> --hold move_z=1`, under a boat 19.07 m
# long drawing 1.65 m — so the top rung is a real sea and not a pond. It is
# also the top: see the measurement on `theedge` itself. (That `--hold` is new.
# Without it `loom water` runs the game with nobody at the wheel, and on a
# scene whose weather rides the boat's position it reports the berth's sea
# however many ticks you give it.)
#
#   arrives sooner    the easing rate in `deeper_rules.rhai`, not here
#   rougher           `wind_speed` on the later rungs — and read `theedge`
#   wetter            `rain_intensity` on the later rungs, 0..100 mm/h
#   dry demo          delete the five `rain_intensity` lines; `Rain.intensity`
#                     below is 0.0, so that is all it takes
#   starts rough      DO NOT raise `inshore`'s `wind_speed` — see `fetch`
#
# **The berth's wind is welded to `WaterBody.fetch` and to thirty taught tapes
# in `green.sh`.** `inshore`'s 3.0 and the fetch of 15 000 m are one number in
# two places; move either alone and the sea at the quay changes, the boarding
# walk re-times, and thirty rows of §6 fail. The full reasoning is on `fetch`.
#
# **Judge the rate at the wheel, not here.** How fast weather *should* arrive
# is a feel decision and nothing in this repository can measure it — the four
# rungs above are checkable and the ramp between them is not.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LOOK — and the three numbers that change it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Every knob is listed in `docs/design/THE-LOOK.md`.** That page is the
# handover; this is the short form.
#
#   brighter          `PIVOT` in `assets/shaders/tonemap.slang` — turn it DOWN
#   more eerie        `saturation` in the last two stages BELOW — turn it UP
#   less of it all    `CONTRAST` in `tonemap.slang` — down toward 1.00, which
#                     is off, and measured: at 1.00 all 54 golden references
#                     come back to inside the gate's own tolerance
#
# **"More eerie" is UP, and it is the one line here that reads backwards.**
# This ladder used to desaturate to 0.26 at its far end, and the measured
# result was a frame that was *dark* rather than *wrong*: the green cast in the
# sea peaked at +11.0 halfway out and fell to +1.03 at the deepest water, so
# the frame the whole mechanic exists to deliver was the least colour-cast rung
# on the ladder. What makes deep water frightening is a colour that should not
# be there. Draining the colour deletes the effect and leaves you with dusk.
# Darkness is `exposure`, and darkness on its own is not the beat — ADR 0071.
#
# The first two numbers are different in kind, and it matters. `PIVOT` is the
# whole engine and re-blesses all 54 references; the stages below are this
# scene alone, and **`deeper_demo` is not in `GOLDEN`**, so every number in the
# ladder is free to retune for ever.
#
# **Judge it in motion.** Nothing in this project can: `flythrough` does not
# move `dread` and `shimmer` holds it still by design. Take the boat out and
# hold W. Measured from the berth, `dread` is 0.000 for the first ten seconds,
# 0.060 at twenty, and runs the whole ladder in the forty after that.
#
# **Play it in a window at least 960 px wide.** Both HUD rows are centre-
# anchored, so a narrower window clips them at both ends. The longest line the
# game can say lays out at 840 px, and
# `loom_cli::hud::the_longest_demo_caption_fits_the_narrowest_documented_window`
# is the test that keeps it that way.
#
# There are **four things in this demo and no fifth**: a hub, a boat you can
# drive, fishing you can do, and four slots to carry things in. It is a demo. It
# is playable end to end and it is not a game.
#
# The hub is the GDD's §7 — a rusted floating platform lashed to the surface
# above the trench, part oil rig, part fishing wharf. Early game it is the cozy
# tonal baseline, so it is bright, warm and low-fog: the "before" the rest of
# the game is measured against.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTROLS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **The title screen says these too**, in two lines along the bottom — see
# `TitleKeys` and `TitleVerbs` at the foot of this file. This section is the
# long form and those are the two lines a player actually reads; when they
# disagree, the ones on screen are the ones that matter, and
# `run::the_demo_opens_on_a_camera_the_game_never_uses` is what stops either
# from quietly emptying.
#
#   on the rig       W A S D walk, SHIFT sprint, SPACE jump
#
#   on `HelmMat`     W ahead, S astern, A wheel to port, D wheel to starboard.
#                    **SPACE is hands off the wheel** and gives you your legs
#                    back. Nothing pressed is neutral; she coasts to a stop.
#
#   anywhere aboard  **CLICK casts, and clicks again to set the hook. SHIFT
#                    reels. The rod is in your hands**, so there is no station
#                    to stand at — cast from the cockpit, the foredeck or the
#                    salon door. Both keys are refused ashore, by never being
#                    sent rather than by a rule that says no.
#
#   in the water     W A S D swim, at under half walking pace. The caption
#                    names the nearer of the two ramps and the compass word for
#                    where it lies, computed from where you are.
#
#   **E**            use what you are standing at, and it is the only key in
#                    this demo that works ashore as well as aboard. What it
#                    does is decided by where you are, in this order:
#
#                      at the crate, with a catch below   the trip closes
#                      at the yellow fish box             it goes below
#                      a fish on the line                 into your hands
#
#   **TAB**          open the creel, and close it. It is a three-by-three grid
#                    and so it is painted rather than written on a line —
#                    `hud::creel`, bottom left. The top HUD row names the key
#                    because nothing else in this demo could.
#
#                    **The world does not stop while it is open**, which is the
#                    whole point of it: a fish on the line is still pulling, a
#                    take still comes and goes, and the boat is still making
#                    way. It takes your legs — W/A/S/D become the cursor — and
#                    it leaves your head, so you can still look around while
#                    your hands are in the bag. TAB on the helm mat is refused
#                    with a word, because there W/A/S/D are the throttle and
#                    the rudder.
#
#                    With it open:
#
#                      W A S D    move the cursor. One tap is one cell; hold to
#                                 repeat, and it stops at the wall
#                      E          lift what the cursor is on — or, holding
#                                 something, put it down there
#                      LMB        turn what you are holding. A 2x3 conger is
#                                 3x2 turned, and sometimes only one of the two
#                                 will go in
#                      SHIFT      hold one second to put a thing over the side.
#                                 What you are holding, or if your hands are
#                                 empty, what the cursor is on. It is the only
#                                 thing in this game you cannot undo
#                      TAB        close it. Anything in your hand goes back
#
#                    A fish that will not fit **stays on the line** and waits
#                    while you make room. Four supplies land in it on the walk
#                    to the boat, so four of the nine cells are gone before you
#                    have caught anything — and the five that are left are not
#                    a rectangle, which is the whole of the puzzle.
#
#   **ESC**          the pause menu — Resume and Quit, over a scrim — and in
#                    its top right, ROOM CODE with a twelve-symbol code under
#                    it, drawn fresh from the operating system every time the
#                    window opens. **Nothing can join it yet.** The code is the
#                    front end of a multiplayer decision that has been made and
#                    not built: host-authoritative with input replication, and
#                    the next step is a second local player, not a socket. See
#                    `docs/decisions/0065-a-room-is-named-before-it-can-be-joined.md`.
#
# **THE CATCH IS SOMETHING YOU DO NOW, AND IT USED TO BE SOMETHING THAT
# HAPPENED.** A landed fish arrived in a slot on the tick it came over the rail
# and was put below because the player had walked near a box. Both were written
# when the engine had five digital channels — `move_x`, `move_z`, `jump`,
# `sprint`, `fire` — and no sixth to bind a verb to, so a *place* was the only
# budget there was. There is a sixth now, `interact` on E, and the whole tail of
# the loop is on it: she hangs off the rod tip until you take her off, and she
# goes below when you ask.
#
# **The helm and the four supplies are deliberately still places.** The helm is
# a stance rather than an action and already has its own way out (SPACE); the
# supplies lie along the one gesture this demo teaches, and putting them on a
# key would mean a first-timer who holds W arrives at the boat with an empty
# hold. Converting either is a decision, not a gap.
#
# **Reel is SHIFT, not W** — W is the throttle at the wheel and your legs
# everywhere else. The cost, stated: aboard, SHIFT is reel *and* still sprint,
# so sprinting round the cockpit with a fish on loads the line. That is a dozen
# ticks of stress on a hundred-point meter.
#
# **SPACE on the mat is the only control here that is not a place**, and it
# exists because the alternative was worse: with no legs on the mat, the only
# way off was jump-plus-a-direction, and that direction was simultaneously the
# wheel — so leaving the helm put you in the sea twelve metres astern of a boat
# you had just spun.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LOOP, IN THE ORDER YOU MEET IT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. You spawn on the rig facing a boat, and **hold W**. Four supplies lie
#      along the walk, alternating sides of the lane, so the taught gesture
#      fills the whole hold on the way. Aboard by tick 125, at the wheel by 191.
#   2. You are at the wheel and under way; channel marks stream past to port
#      about one every 2.7 s, which is how you can see that you are moving.
#      `Rig/Boat.x` reaches 29.89 by tick 900.
#   3. **SPACE.** You have let go of the wheel and the caption changes.
#   4. **CLICK.** The rod in your hands casts — from wherever you are standing,
#      because it is in your hands. Wait, and a fish takes it — the float goes
#      clean under for the 600 ms the take is answerable in.
#   5. **CLICK again inside that window** to set the hook, then hold SHIFT to
#      reel and let go when she runs. The blank bends with the load: all
#      twenty-four joints read `state.stress`. **Off beats on** — every cadence
#      that lands a fish has more release than pull, which is the skill.
#   6. Land it and **she hangs off the rod tip**, swinging, with the line still
#      through her. **Press E** and she comes off into your hands — into the
#      slot the bait she ate has just freed, and the bottom row shows `[ ><> ]`
#      where that bait was. SPACE, then follow the caption: it names the yellow
#      fish box in metres in *your* frame, and the bait box is between you and
#      it, so the walk is a dogleg. **E again at the box** and she goes below.
#
#      With all four slots full she stays on the line and the caption says so.
#      That is recoverable rather than a loss: E at the fish box takes a catch
#      off the line as well as out of your hands, so the box is always the way
#      out of a full hold. Nothing is ever thrown back.
#   7. Take her home. **You left ahead, so astern is how you come back**, and
#      the helm caption carries `HOME nn m — hold S`. Step off over the boarding
#      steps and walk west along the wharf to the crate. **E**, and `CRATE` goes
#      up. Then take bait and go again.
#
# The bait is what makes it a loop rather than a checklist: the fish eats yours
# at the take, and there is none at sea.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHE IS A SOLID OBJECT NOW, AND SHE WAS NOT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Three things a player found in one sitting, and they were one bug: the hull
# was **drawn** solid and **collided** as a lid with a deckhouse-shaped brick on
# it. The hull's own `BoxCollider` is demoted to mass-only the moment
# `jib_vi_decks.loom` attaches a deck plate (ADR 0060) — which is right, or you
# stand on an invisible lid a metre above the deck — and every box in that
# prefab started at or above y = 0.30 against a waterline of 0.
#
#   * **You could swim through her.** Not leak through: *through*. Two metres
#     off her port topside, holding one key, a swimmer crossed the hull at a
#     flat 1.9 m/s and came out thirteen metres the other side. Seven underbody
#     boxes now carry her section from the keel up to the sheer.
#   * **You could not get into the salon**, which is the best-looking room on
#     the boat and visible through her windows the whole time. Her after
#     bulkhead has a 0.92 m door in it and 2.2 m of headroom behind it; the
#     collider was one solid block, authored on a headroom measurement taken
#     off the *previous* hull mesh.
#   * **You could not get back aboard.** `Rig/Boat/SternLadder` is the answer
#     and it is the only thing here that is new geometry rather than corrected
#     collision. The caption in the water names it when it is the nearer way
#     out, which at the fishing ground it always is — the rig is 88 m away.
#
# Each has a row in `scripts/green.sh` and a scene of its own:
# `rig_underhull.loom`, `rig_salon.loom`, `rig_reboard.loom`.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR DECISIONS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU CHANGE ANYTHING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **1. What the player is.** `Rig/Player` is a `CharacterController` 1.8 m tall,
# driven by `../scripts/deeper_player.rhai`, with `Rig/Player/Eye` carrying the
# `Camera` as its child. That parenting is not decoration:
# `World::player_character` walks *up* from the active camera to the nearest
# ancestor with a `CharacterController`, so **parenting the camera to the
# character is what makes it the player**, and it is also what makes the rig
# first person. There is no `Player` flag component and there must not be one.
#
# The movement script is not `fps.rhai` because that one detonates a 5 m blast
# where the view ray lands on `fire`, and the centrepiece of this scene is a
# 57,636 kg dynamic rigid body. `fire` arrives here unspent, for fishing.
#
# **The scene is turned, not the camera.** `Play::start` takes its opening yaw
# from the eye node's authored rotation and then writes yaw onto the character,
# leaving the eye's own yaw underneath — so an authored eye yaw is counted twice
# and an authored character yaw is overwritten. Neither can express "start
# facing this way". So the berth is on −Z, which is where yaw 0 looks, and
# `loom render` and `loom run` agree about the opening shot. (That double-count
# is a real engine wrinkle and worth an issue; it was not worth an engine change
# inside a demo round.)
#
# **The view has weight.** Yaw runs through a damped spring with a feedforward
# term (`CameraSpring` in `play.rs`), so a turn accelerates from rest, arrives
# about 3° past where you flicked, and settles in 117 ms. The feedforward is the
# part that keeps it weight rather than lag: a sustained turn ends up 5.2 ms
# behind the mouse, less than the 16.7 ms a mouse event already waits for a
# tick. Stepped on the fixed tick, so every machine gets the same camera
# whatever its frame rate. Pitch is deliberately rigid — on a boat a vertically
# swimming horizon is indistinguishable from the sea moving, which is the one
# artifact this scene cannot afford.
#
# **The picture is never more than 6° from the mouse**, whatever you do with it.
# A spring's overshoot is proportional to how fast you were turning, and without
# that bound an ordinary flick threw the view 18° past where it was pointed and
# a fast spin 47°. At the speeds you look around a deck the clamp does nothing
# at all — the two are bit-identical — so what it removes is only the part that
# was never weight.
#
# **Tuning it, between two runs of the same binary:**
#
#     LOOM_CAMERA_HZ=3          heavier. Lower is heavier; 2 is the floor.
#     LOOM_CAMERA_HZ=5          sharper. 6 is the ceiling, and by then there
#                               is almost nothing left to feel.
#     LOOM_CAMERA_DAMPING=0.45  more of a lurch at the end of a turn. 1.0 is
#                               none at all.
#     LOOM_CAMERA_WEIGHT=0      off, exactly — how to tell whether it is
#                               better or worse than nothing.
#
# Out-of-range values are clamped and say so in the log. **`HZ` is the one knob
# worth moving**; the rest are there to answer a specific question rather than
# to be dialled in. Whether any of this is *right* is not something a test can
# say — it needs a person, a window and a rail to walk along.
#
# **2. Where the four slots live.** In `GameRules` state, as `state.hold` and
# `state.carried`, owned by `../scripts/deeper_rules.rhai` — the alternatives
# (a movement script's `memory`, the event log) are weighed and rejected in that
# file's header. Short version: `memory` is invisible to both `loom sim --assert`
# and the `Hud`, and the event log is a record rather than a container.
#
# The slots are also four named numbers — `bait`, `line`, `lantern`, `thermos` —
# beside `infish`, `stowed` and `full`, with the events `pickup`, `spend`,
# `stow`, `refused` and `deliver`. **The cost, stated:** `state` is not in
# `state_hash`, so `cargo test` and `cargo xtask repeat` are blind to the hold
# and `--assert` is its only detector. Same bargain the fight makes.
#
# **3. The sea is the safety device, not the railing.** The rig was sealed on
# four sides in round 1 and the seal leaked anyway: walk into any rail at
# walking pace, keep pressing, and hop — the capsule's rounded foot catches the
# cap bar's inner edge at the jump apex and climbs over. A taller rail cannot
# fix it (the height a solid wall would need is above the player's own eye) and
# the berth has to open anyway or nobody can board. So instead:
# `deeper_player.rhai` **floats** a capsule that goes under, the slipway at the
# east end lets it walk back out, and `assets/test/rig_overboard.loom` asserts
# both with the real player model and no teleport. The rails are set dressing.
#
# **4. The helm is a place, and the layout exists so nobody has to be told.**
# The player spawns at x = −3.70 facing the gap in the berth railing; the gap is
# where the boat's cockpit is; the cockpit is where `HelmMat` is drawn. Walk
# forward and you board her, arrive at the wheel and get under way, on one key.
# The boarding lane is **24 degrees wide** — `move_x` from −0.21 to +0.21 all
# reach the wheel by tick 600 — and the whole hold fills across −0.15 to +0.15.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * **Selling, money, progression, quests, NPCs, day/night.** Out of scope. This
#   is four features and no fifth.
# * **A second fishing spot, a second boat, a second fish species.** The demo
#   proves the loop, not its variety.
# * **A line from the rod tip to the float, or to the fish hanging off it.** It
#   is a second node whose length is a scale, and it is the obvious next thing —
#   more obvious now than it was, because the rod is in your hands and its tip
#   is a metre and a half from the camera rather than eight metres away. At six
#   metres to the float a real one is thinner than a pixel; to the fish it would
#   not be.
# * **A dead man's handle.** Not needed: leaving the mat makes `at_helm` false
#   while `aboard` stays true, so `deeper_player.rhai` writes zero thrust on the
#   next tick. Measured on `rig_drive` with `--hold "jump=1,move_z=1"`: x = 3.0002
#   at tick 300, 2.9998 at 900, 3.0004 at 1800, from a berth at 3.0.
# * **Moving wheel, throttle and shifter.** All four movable parts in
#   `jib_vi.loom` are separate meshes with no transform of their own, so each
#   one's pivot is the hull's origin up to nineteen metres away and rotating the
#   node swings the part across the boat. The *scripting* half is solved — a node
#   `Script` reads the rules script's numbers, which is what bends the rod — so
#   what is missing is mesh work and only mesh work.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN ISSUES — what a player will hit that is NOT fixed
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Ranked by how likely a first-timer is to meet it. None is a stopper; all are
# seams.
#
# 0. **Nobody has watched the title screen move.** `loom run` needs a real
#    window and this box has no Xvfb. What *is* checked: the shot is rendered
#    and looked at; the curtain's curve, the menu's words, the layer it paints
#    into, its refusal to eat clicks and its refusal to answer one before the
#    opening lifts are unit tests; and both fades have been composited frame by
#    frame over the real render at the alphas the code produces, with egui's own
#    Ubuntu-Light at the real point sizes — which answers the two questions that
#    were open (96 pt reads as a title, not as a debug overlay; the lift is even
#    rather than nothing-then-slam).
#
#    **That compositing had to be redone in linear light and the first pass was
#    wrong.** The overlay blends premultiplied into an `_SRGB` swapchain, so the
#    hardware blends in linear — doing it on the bytes made the fade look twice
#    as dark at its halfway point and would have argued for retuning a curve
#    that is correct. It is the same class of error as measuring anti-aliasing
#    on a path the player never sees.
#
#    Still unanswered, and only a window can answer them: the *pace* of
#    0.5 / 0.25 / 0.8 (the shape is proven, the durations are judgement);
#    whether building this 214-node world fits inside the quarter second of
#    black, and whether the first gameplay frame's pipeline warm-up lands
#    inside it; whether the pointer hand-off under the curtain is clean;
#    whether a static camera over a moving sea reads as alive or as a still;
#    and the layout at window sizes other than 960x600, since the word's offset
#    is absolute pixels. Three durations, one scrim and one offset are the
#    knobs, all in `loom_cli::hud`; expect to move them.
# 1. **You drive home stern-first at a wharf you cannot see.** The berth faces
#    the way it faces, so the leg the demo teaches leaves ahead and returns
#    astern with the destination behind the camera. Photographed at the arrival
#    beat: sea, sky and a rod, with the rig 180 degrees behind. The caption
#    answers it with a range and a key, which is the cheap half. The real fix is
#    geometric — berth her bow-in, or give the helm a stern view — and it turns
#    every pinned drive number in `scripts/green.sh` from an X into a Z, and
#    lands the helmsman a metre from the after face of the deckhouse. That is a
#    round, not a bullet.
# 2. **The landed fish reads as a mitten.** It tracks your hands correctly and
#    it is in your own frame now rather than off her port beam, so turning no
#    longer swings it out over the sea. What is left is the mesh: a grey-green
#    body with five splayed rays. Mesh work, not a scene edit.
#
#    **And it is still attached to nothing**, in both poses. On the line it
#    hangs at the rod tip with a metre of air where the line should be; in your
#    hands there is no hand. See DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
# 3. **Every close render is dense salt-and-pepper.** That is ADR 0019's
#    eight-ray AO dither. Two sweeps found no scene-level knob that reaches it —
#    ambient is flat across 2.4x, the sun about a ninth of it — and the cure is
#    the quad-wide sample share in that ADR, which moves all 54 references.
# 4. **A hard turn to port stops the boat.** The channel marks are static
#    colliders nine and a half metres to port — the side the demo teaches you to
#    watch — so the same input mirrored has two outcomes. `state.pinned` says so
#    in the caption (`PUSHING ON SOMETHING   S to back off`) but does **not** cut
#    the throttle: she grinds on the buoy until you press S. Cutting it is a
#    force-path change and wants its own ADR.
# 5. **`state.aboard` is stale by about eighty ticks** after you step ashore, so
#    the arrival caption uses the aboard branch while you stand on the wharf.
#    Both branches now say the same useful thing, so it is cosmetic.
# 6. **FIXED 2026-09-06. A hard turn used to bounce the helmsman off the mat**,
#    and it had stopped being the harmless flicker recorded here: off the mat
#    the held W is his legs rather than the throttle, so it walked him further
#    out — 0.37 m in 20 ticks against 0.02 m in the previous 140 — and by tick
#    2400 he was off the boat entirely. `dread` froze with the hull, which is
#    what `green.sh`'s offshore wind-and-rain row was failing on. At the helm
#    his legs do not exist, so the only thing that can move him off the mat is
#    the boat: `deeper_player.rhai` now holds the mat until *he* lets go.
#    See ADR 0075 Addendum 1. The slide itself is unchanged and unexplained.
# 7. **Hold W and SPACE together from the spawn and you wedge for ever.** SPACE
#    on the mat is hands-off, so the throttle never opens and the pair pushes
#    into the cockpit geometry. Releasing either key clears it.
# 8. **Wedged aboard with an empty hold, `BLOCKED` still says `go round it`**
#    without naming what has you. There are **thirty-seven** colliders on that
#    hull now and nobody has worked out which. The likeliest new one is the
#    salon door: press starboard-and-slightly-aft while standing in it and the
#    capsule can catch the jamb, which is why the collider gap is 1.12 m
#    against a drawn opening of 0.92 — see `jib_vi_decks.loom`.
# 9. **The deck is beige and the hub reads pale.** A palette pass, not a boat.
# 10. **You walk through the settees, the tables and the wheelhouse console.**
#    The deckhouse is collided as a shell and its furniture is not, on the
#    grounds that colliding the console costs the wheelhouse outright: the
#    passages either side of the drawn one are 0.14 m clear against a 0.70 m
#    capsule. So the salon is a room you can walk into and the fittings in it
#    are painted on. `jib_vi_decks.loom` states the trade where the boxes are.
# 11. **The rod is a view-model and clips.** It is posed 0.55 m in front of the
#    eye and 0.45 m below it — a first-person cheat, because the anatomically
#    right grip at chest height is 76 degrees below a 37.5 degree half-FOV and
#    entirely off the bottom of the frame. Two metres of blank at that offset
#    reaches 2.3 m ahead of the eye, so **anything nearer than that is inside
#    it**, and nothing depth-biases a view-model here.
#
#    **Stated from the geometry, not from a reproduction.** Two attempts to
#    walk into the deckhouse and photograph it went round the outside instead;
#    what is measured is the offset and the blank length, not a screenshot of
#    the artifact.
#
# 12. **The rod does not point where you look in pitch, only in yaw.** A rules
#    script publishes the player's flat heading and nothing else, so the blank
#    sits at a fixed 60 degrees of elevation whether you are looking at the sky
#    or at your feet. It reads fine because a real rod is held at an angle to
#    the eye anyway, and fixing it means publishing a pitch nothing else wants.
#
# 13. **The stern ladder adds 3.4 m to her length and it is always there.**
#    A 45 degree ramp from y = -1.80 to her transom cap is 3.42 m of run and
#    nothing shortens it (that file's block says what else was measured). A
#    real one folds; this one cannot, because a node script may write a
#    transform and there is no key left to ask it with.
#
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THIS IS TESTED
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Section 6 of `scripts/green.sh` — 18 scenes and 7 blocks of deliberately wrong
# input, about two minutes. It runs the whole trip on **this file** from a
# keyboard tape (`--hold "<tick>:<keys>"`, the same field `loom run` writes when
# a human holds a key) and asserts `state.delivered == 1` at the end of it.
#
# **The tape now presses E three times and the ticks are measured, not chosen.**
# `DEMO_FIGHT` / `DEMO_BOX` / `DEMO_HELM` / `DEMO_HOME` are declared once in
# that file and nest; eight rows used to carry a verbatim copy of the same
# forty-key cadence. Aiming a press also found a stale claim: the comment on the
# `rig_trip` row said the catch was stowed at tick 1310 on the walk across the
# cockpit, and the closest that walk ever comes to the hatch is **2.08 m**
# against a `RANGE` of 2.0 — the proximity stow it described was actually firing
# on the way ashore, six hundred ticks later. A place fires wherever you happen
# to be; a key has to be aimed, so it says where you are.
#
# `assets/test/rig_*.loom` are **not forks.** Each is an `extends` of this file —
# `rig_trip.loom` is 16 non-comment lines, `rig_adrift.loom` is 24 — adding one
# node and inheriting everything else, so they cannot drift from it. An earlier
# note here called them "1,900-line forks" and that was simply wrong.
# `rig_trip.loom` adds a `Pilot` so the fight plays itself while a tape sails the
# boat; `rig_adrift.loom` leaves her 21 m off the crate and swims the player home,
# which is the control that proves the crate refuses a hull that is not alongside.
#
# **What no gate can see:** the overlay. `Hud` draws only in `loom run`, so a
# headless render shows neither row, and a `grep` on `game.message` proves a
# string was produced — never that it was legible or drawn. The one measurement
# that exists is the width test named at the top of this file.
#
[scene]
format = 1
id = "b2a43f8f-314a-4d92-b4aa-322411869fcd"

# The hull, its paint and its sixteen pontoons, from the file that owns them.
[[prefab]]
key = "jib_vi"
id = "3c7a5e12-8b64-4f09-a2d3-6e1b90c47f58"
path = "../prefabs/jib_vi.loom"

# The deck a person stands on. See that file's header: the hull's own collider
# is its inertia and its lid is a metre above the deck, so without this the
# boat is a brick you stand on top of.
[[prefab]]
key = "jib_vi_decks"
id = "3c7a5e12-8b64-4f09-a2d3-6e1b90c47f59"
path = "../prefabs/jib_vi_decks.loom"

# The rod and its reel, from the file that owns them. Twenty-four of its nodes
# carry `../scripts/rod_bend.rhai`, so it loads up when the fight does, and its
# root carries `../scripts/rod_hold.rhai`, so it is in the player's hands.
[[prefab]]
key = "rod"
id = "7f2c14a9-63d8-4e51-b0a7-2d9e5c81f430"
path = "../prefabs/fishing_rod.loom"

# **The man himself** — 43 nodes, 27 rigid mesh leaves, 4,140 triangles. Loom
# has no skeletal animation, so he is an articulated puppet on a node chain and
# fourteen node `Script`s bend him. See "THE BODY AND THE GLASS", below.
[[prefab]]
key = "deckhand"
id = "7a41c0de-5b2e-4f18-9d63-2c8ae5f10b47"
path = "../prefabs/deckhand.loom"

# The channel marks. See "THE MARKS", below.
[[prefab]]
key = "buoy"
id = "1a6f42d7-58b0-4e93-9c21-7d4b6e0f3a85"
path = "../prefabs/channel_buoy.loom"

# The catch, in the two meshes it ships as: `_chrome` is the body and
# `_coral` is every fin and the tail. Rendering only the body gives a pale
# featureless lozenge, which is what the first attempt looked like.
# 0.10 m nose to tail as it ships, so `fish_hang.rhai` scales it.
[[asset]]
key = "gleamsprat"
id = "6537b1f4-2257-44de-9ba2-a4b0b56d7085"
path = "../meshes/gleamsprat_chrome.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "gleamsprat_fins"
id = "b3a90c47-1f52-4d68-9e04-5c7218ab3d61"
path = "../meshes/gleamsprat_coral.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "box"
path = "box"

[[asset]]
key = "cylinder"
path = "cylinder"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WORLD
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[[node]]
name = "Rig"

  [node.components.GameRules]
  path = "../scripts/deeper_rules.rhai"

  # Bright, warm, low sun. The GDD asks the hub to be the cozy baseline, and
  # every later escalation is a change to these seven numbers rather than to
  # the geometry.
  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.42, 0.46, -0.78]
  sun_strength = 1.25
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.93, 0.80]
  ambient = 0.45
  sky_zenith = [0.16, 0.30, 0.52]
  sky_horizon = [0.74, 0.72, 0.66]
  fog_density = 0.0028
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # The mood ladder — ADR 0069. `deeper_rules.rhai` eases `state.dread` from
  # the boat's distance off the rig, on the fixed tick, and every number below
  # walks between its neighbours as it moves.
  #
  # A stage's `[..environment]` is a PATCH: anything it does not name is the
  # block above, and anything the block above does not name either is the
  # component default. That is why `sun_direction` appears once in this file —
  # the title shot is composed against that vector and a sign flip on z turns
  # the rig into a small beige object on flat grey water.
  #
  # **That last rung of the fallback used to be missing, and it stepped.**
  # `inshore` names no patch at all and this block names no `cloud_cover`, so
  # the whole near half of the ladder held a clear sky and then snapped to 0.30
  # the tick `dread` crossed 0.25 — a cloud deck out of nothing. Measured at
  # 320x200, the same 0.0001 of `dread`: mean 3.115 across the boundary against
  # 0.0032 just below it. Fixed in `mood_of`; ADR 0071.
  dread = 0.0

    [[node.components.Environment.stages]]
    name = "inshore"
    at = 0.0
    # The floor, and it is the scene's own `Wind.speed` exactly — see `fetch`.
    wind_speed = 3.0
    rain_intensity = 0.0
    # The rig at dawn, and the only stage whose grade ADDS. A warm tilt and a
    # saturation lift with `contrast` left neutral: ADR 0068's operator already
    # moves this frame's median up a few codes, and "not too bright" was the
    # brief — the near end must not add to that.
      [node.components.Environment.stages.grade]
      gain = [1.02, 1.00, 0.98]
      contrast = 1.00
      saturation = 1.12

    [[node.components.Environment.stages]]
    name = "underway"
    at = 0.25
    wind_speed = 4.5
    rain_intensity = 1.5
    # Still a nice day. The only thing that has happened is that the light has
    # gone slightly flat and the sky has lost a little of its blue.
      [node.components.Environment.stages.environment]
      sun_strength = 1.00
      sun_color = [0.98, 0.95, 0.86]
      ambient = 0.42
      sky_zenith = [0.15, 0.26, 0.40]
      sky_horizon = [0.66, 0.66, 0.60]
      fog_density = 0.009
      cloud_cover = 0.30
      exposure = 0.96
      [node.components.Environment.stages.grade]
      gain = [1.00, 1.00, 0.98]
      contrast = 1.00
      saturation = 0.98

    [[node.components.Environment.stages]]
    name = "outofsight"
    at = 0.5
    wind_speed = 7.0
    rain_intensity = 6.0
    # **The wrongness beat, and it is NOT dark — the third-brightest rung.**
    # The sea has gone the colour of an aquarium nobody has cleaned while the
    # deck you are standing on is still lit.
    #
    # The green is `sky_zenith`, and blue is the trap. At matched luminance
    # teal reads as dusk and is rather beautiful — it is Abzu — grey reads as a
    # dull day and rust reads as a sunset. Desaturated yellow-green is the only
    # one of the four that is unpleasant, and it only lands paired with the
    # overcast ceiling and the saturation drop; on its own it reads smoggy.
      [node.components.Environment.stages.environment]
      sun_strength = 0.75
      sun_color = [0.92, 0.96, 0.78]
      ambient = 0.36
      sky_zenith = [0.13, 0.20, 0.16]
      sky_horizon = [0.52, 0.56, 0.40]
      fog_density = 0.018
      fog_falloff = 0.04
      cloud_cover = 0.55
      exposure = 0.88
      [node.components.Environment.stages.grade]
      gain = [0.96, 1.00, 0.90]
      contrast = 1.02
      saturation = 0.72

    [[node.components.Environment.stages]]
    name = "grounds"
    at = 0.8
    wind_speed = 11.5
    rain_intensity = 16.0
      [node.components.Environment.stages.environment]
      sun_strength = 0.40
      sun_color = [0.80, 0.92, 0.70]
      ambient = 0.30
      sky_zenith = [0.07, 0.11, 0.09]
      sky_horizon = [0.24, 0.30, 0.22]
      fog_density = 0.034
      fog_falloff = 0.03
      cloud_cover = 0.85
      exposure = 0.80
      [node.components.Environment.stages.grade]
      gain = [0.88, 0.97, 0.86]
      contrast = 1.06
      saturation = 0.62

    [[node.components.Environment.stages]]
    name = "theedge"
    at = 1.0
    # **The top, and it is 16.0 because that is where the DECK gives out.**
    #
    # It said 5.0, and the measurement behind that varied two things at once.
    # The sweep it quoted set every rung FLAT to one speed and read green.sh
    # §6's long-turn row:
    #
    #     flat rung   3.0  3.5  4.0  4.5  5.0  5.5  6.0
    #     full wheel  PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS FAIL FAIL
    #
    # But a flat rung also raises the *berth* wind, and the `fetch` note above
    # says in as many words that the berth must not move: thirty tapes in this
    # file are calibrated on a boarding walk that grinds on that exact sea, and
    # the failures were the helmsman arriving at the wheel at a different tick,
    # not the sea taking him off the mat. Re-run raising only THIS rung, berth
    # held at 3.0, same three rows verbatim from `green.sh`:
    #
    #     top rung        5.0  8.0 10.0 12.0 16.0 20.0
    #     turn, 3200     PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS
    #     wind, 3000     PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS
    #     steam, 1800    PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS PASS
    #
    # **The whole of green.sh §6 + §7 passes with this rung at 16.0** — every
    # one of the 114 lines in that file that names this scene, the
    # three-process byte-identity check included.
    #
    # **But there IS a ceiling, and it is his footing, not the hull's.** Past
    # the gate's longest tape, holding W to the far edge of the played water:
    #
    #     top rung    16      17      18      19      20
    #     at tick   3400   at_helm 1   0       0       0       0
    #     boat x     153     148     145     145     127
    #
    # `at_helm` going to zero is the helmsman sliding out of the mat rectangle
    # in the BOAT's frame — `on_mat_now` in `deeper_player.rhai` — with the
    # hull inside a few centimetres of still water throughout. It is not the
    # boat failing. At 16 he holds the wheel the whole trip and comes off at
    # tick 3600, by which point she has stopped at x = 158 anyway. At 17 he is
    # off before the trip is done. The crossing is between 16 and 17.
    #
    # **A second instrument, on a different scene, lands in the same place.**
    # `assets/test/weather_ramp.loom` is this ladder over the five-station
    # carry rig, and ADR 0060's acceptance is 30 mm peak-to-peak in the boat
    # frame. Its riders swept over ticks 1800-2100, by top rung:
    #
    #     Wind.speed   10     12     14     16     18     20
    #     worst p-p  12.9   19.2   23.6   23.9   33.6   38.5  mm
    #
    # Monotone, crossing between 16 and 18. Two measurements of the same organ
    # — a passenger drifting on a heaving deck — from two directions, agreeing.
    # 16 keeps a fifth of that budget.
    #
    # **Raising it further is ADR 0060 work, not weather work**: platform carry
    # is upward-only and ratchets, and that is the organ to fix before this
    # number moves again. ADR 0075 §4.
    wind_speed = 16.0
    rain_intensity = 30.0
    # Sea and sky are one value: there is no `out there` any more and the world
    # stops at forty metres.
    #
    # **This rung is not the darkest thing the ladder can do, deliberately —
    # ADR 0071.** It used to be, and being darkest is what made it stop being
    # frightening. Four dimmers were turned at once here: `sun_strength`,
    # `ambient`, `exposure` and the grade's `saturation`. Measured over nine
    # points on the axis, the green cast in the sea band ran +11.00 at
    # `outofsight` and back down to **+1.03** here, and whole-frame saturation
    # 0.196 -> 0.063 — so the deepest water was the least colour-cast rung on
    # the ladder, and it read as nightfall rather than as wrongness. It now
    # holds +8.28.
    #
    # The deck went with it too: 0.48 of sky luma at the berth down to 0.23,
    # landing at luma 30 — too dark to work on, against this design's own words
    # at `outofsight`, "while the deck you are standing on is still lit". It
    # lands at 43 now.
    #
    # **`saturation` is the knob, and it goes UP.** Measured here at dread 1.0,
    # 0.50 -> 0.75 moves the sea's cast +4.38 and the frame's brightness -0.1:
    # it changes the colour and nothing else, which is exactly what this rung
    # needs and why it must not be turned together with the dimmers.
    #
    # `exposure` is the number you would not guess. The cloud deck's tones are
    # constants in `scene.slang`, so turning the sun down does not darken an
    # overcast sky — at `sun_strength = 0` the frame moves by a fraction of a
    # code. `exposure` is applied in the tonemap, downstream of the deck, and
    # it is what actually takes this rung down. It is also the only dimmer that
    # should be doing it: 0.66 -> 0.85 is +17.6 luma and almost no cast.
    #
    # `fog_density` is nearly inert at this rung and that surprised the
    # measurement — 0.058 -> 0.090 buys +0.5 luma, because at `cloud_cover =
    # 1.0` the frame is already fog-limited. Fog does its work in the middle
    # rungs.
      [node.components.Environment.stages.environment]
      sun_strength = 0.12
      sun_color = [0.66, 0.84, 0.62]
      ambient = 0.22
      sky_zenith = [0.03, 0.055, 0.042]
      sky_horizon = [0.10, 0.15, 0.11]
      fog_density = 0.058
      fog_falloff = 0.02
      cloud_cover = 1.00
      exposure = 0.66
      [node.components.Environment.stages.grade]
      gain = [0.84, 1.00, 0.82]
      contrast = 1.10
      saturation = 0.50

  # Light air. Enough to move the surface; far too little to trouble the boat.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 20.0
  speed = 3.0
  gustiness = 0.25
  turbulence = 0.15

  # **The shower. This component's own `intensity` is never used** — every rung
  # of the ladder above names a `rain_intensity`, and a named rung wins. It is
  # here because a mood stage cannot conjure rain: `weather_at` ramps the rate
  # of a `Rain` the scene authored and leaves a scene without one dry, so that
  # rain always arrives with a duration, an audio bed and a collision bake
  # rather than as three quiet absences. Zero, so that deleting the five
  # `rain_intensity` lines gives a dry demo rather than a permanent downpour.
  #
  # `duration` is deliberately absent. The shower here is not on a clock, it is
  # on the ladder — it comes in as you steam out and it goes when you turn for
  # home, because `dread` is a function of where the boat is.
  [node.components.Rain]
  intensity = 0.0

# **`deterministic`, not `cinematic`.** ADR 0053's cinematic tier is read back
# off the GPU and `loom sim` refuses the whole run rather than answer a `water@`
# assertion approximately. A scene the demo's tests are run against gets the
# height-field tier; the beauty shot can have its own file.
[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Rig"

  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  simulation = "deterministic"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1000.0
  drag = 2.0
  # **Fifteen kilometres of open water upwind, and the number is chosen to sit
  # just past a threshold rather than for its own sake.**
  #
  # Without a fetch the derived sea is fully-developed Pierson-Moskowitz, which
  # is SCALE-INVARIANT: `A` goes as `U²` and `k` as `U⁻²`, so slope is the same
  # at every wind speed there is. Measured on `ocean.loom` with its waves
  # stripped, realised `mu_max` over a 9x9 grid runs 0.112 / 0.105 / 0.090 /
  # 0.047 at Wind.speed 3 / 8 / 12 / 16 — the sea gets *smoother* as the wind
  # rises — while the longest wave runs 8 m to 238 m. Raising the wind on that
  # sea is a zoom knob, so the ladder below would move nothing worth seeing.
  #
  # **Why 15 000 and not 3 000.** A fetch-limited sea grows into the
  # fully-developed one at `18_906·U10²/g` metres, which at this scene's calm
  # rung is 14 310 m. 15 000 is just past it, so at `dread = 0` the berth is
  # *exactly* the Pierson-Moskowitz sea this demo has always opened on —
  # measured, `Hs = 0.16592977941036224` before and after, bit for bit — and
  # every one of the twenty-one boarding, creel and helm rows in green.sh §6
  # is untouched. Below the crossing the berth would be Hs 0.076, half the sea
  # the whole demo was authored and gated on, and **that halving re-times the
  # boarding walk**: arrival at the wheel moves from tick 360 to 200 and thirty
  # gate rows fail. The crossing is 5% away, so this number and `Wind.speed`
  # at the `inshore` rung must move together or the berth changes underneath
  # every tape in the file.
  fetch = 15000.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOU
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Deck top is 1.40 and the capsule's origin is its centre, 0.90 above its feet,
# so 2.30 is standing. 2.40 is a 10 cm drop onto the deck on the first tick,
# which is how every character scene in this repository starts: it proves the
# floor is there rather than assuming it.
[[node]]
name = "Player"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [-4.30, 2.40, 0.90] }

  [node.components.CharacterController]
  height = 1.8
  radius = 0.35
  # **0.10, not the default 0.35, and this is the other half of the rail.**
  # `step_height` is what the controller will silently lift the capsule onto,
  # and it applies in mid-air: at the top of a hop the capsule's feet are
  # within a step of the rail cap, so the controller mounts it, walks the
  # 0.2 m cap and drops off the far side into the sea. Measured, on
  # `rig_walk` — the pilot crossed x = 11.50 at y = 3.61, rose to 3.75 over
  # the next eight ticks, and was at y = -970 by tick 900.
  #
  # Nothing on this rig is a stair, so the step buys nothing here and costs
  # that. **The boarding round will want it back** — the bulwark is a 0.22 m
  # rise from the wharf — and when it raises this number it has to re-check
  # the rail against the same sum: rail top must exceed the jump apex's foot
  # height plus this. Today that is 2.733 + 0.10 against a cap at 2.90.
  step_height = 0.25

  [node.components.Script]
  path = "../scripts/deeper_player.rhai"

# **0.82, and it was 0.75 until he had a head.** The eye sits inside the closed
# dome of the sou'wester and the head vanishes for free — the opaque pipeline
# is `CullModeFlags::BACK` and an eye inside a closed shell sees only
# backfaces. **The BRIM does not vanish**, because it is a horizontal surface
# and the eye is above it, so you see its underside. Measured at 0.75 the ochre
# wedge covered the bottom 18% of the frame looking sideways and about 45%
# looking back over his shoulder, where the sou'wester's rear sweep is 30%
# longer. At 0.82 it is gone at 90 degrees and a rim along the bottom fifth at
# 180 — a helmet edge, which is what a hat in first person is supposed to look
# like.
#
# The alternative was a `visible` flag on `MeshRenderer`, and it is refused for
# the reason it would have been added: hiding a part from the raster path would
# have to hide it from the TLAS too, and the mirror would then show a headless
# man.
[[node]]
name = "Eye"
parent = "Rig/Player"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 0.82, 0.0] }

  [node.components.Camera]
  fov_y_degrees = 75.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BODY AND THE GLASS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **`pos = [0, -0.90, 0]` and it is not a nudge.** The prefab's origin is the
# SOLE PLANE — y 0 is the deck he stands on — and a `CharacterController`'s
# origin is its capsule CENTRE, 0.90 m above his feet, which is the same fact
# `Eye`'s +0.75 is measured from. Mount him at identity and he floats.
#
# **It could not exist at all until `play.rs` learned `character_ancestor`.** A
# `MeshRenderer` with no *dynamic* ancestor becomes a static collider from its
# drawn bounds, and a `CharacterController` is not a dynamic body — so all 27
# leaves used to bake as invisible scenery at the spawn point and the player
# walked into his own chest. That is the same engine wrinkle `Rig/Boat/Rod`'s
# header describes as the reason the rod is parented to the hull; with the fix
# in, the rod could now be parented to `.../HandR` and posed in his own frame
# instead of hers. Deliberately not done in this round.
#
# **He is invisible from inside.** The pipeline is `CullModeFlags::BACK` and the
# near plane is 0.1 m, so an eye inside the closed hat dome sees backfaces and
# the head is simply not there — no `visible` flag, and there must not be one:
# hiding a part from the raster path would have to hide it from the TLAS too,
# which is exactly what breaks the mirror it would have been added for.
#
# Nothing here is a component on the instance. `prefab::expand` keeps a node's
# `transform` and its `[node.overrides]` and DISCARDS its `components`, so the
# fourteen joint scripts are attached inside `deckhand.loom` — the same reason
# `rod_hold.rhai` lives inside `fishing_rod.loom`.
[[node]]
name = "Body"
parent = "Rig/Player"
prefab = "deckhand"
transform = { pos = [0.0, -0.90, 0.0] }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RIG
#
# A 24 x 14 m deck on six pilings, railed the whole way round. Every
# piece here is a `box` or a `cylinder` with a `MeshRenderer` and no collider:
# a renderable node with no dynamic ancestor becomes a static collider from its
# own drawn bounds, so what is walked on is exactly what is seen. That
# equivalence is the whole reason nothing here is an invisible ramp.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

